## TASK
Deliver the request end to end inside {{root}}, as the roles of the spine would: product, design, acceptance, craft, review. Work through the tool: start with `next` and follow it. The skills that `next` lists are part of the step. Every step says what it is for, which fields it needs (with a valid example for each: `nwstep record STEP --help --workspace WS`) and which skills to read BEFORE it. A refusal prints WHAT, WHY and HOW: supply the content its <placeholders> mark, then run the HOW line once, exactly as printed; do not re-run the same record unchanged. A refused command shows its last output lines in the refusal, and the full output is kept in the workspace. The tool runs your RED, refactor check, mutant, scorecard, GREEN and probe commands itself, reads the test counts and timings from their output, reads the git history of the change, reads the failing test ids, and compares the scorecard with the pre-feature tree: never type a count, a timing, a file list, a commit list, a test id or a finding count, the tool derives them.
- Request numbering: run `nwstep sentences --file REQUEST` before the oracle. The sentence numbers S1..Sn are the tool's; map those numbers, not a count you made yourself.
- Oracle first (one record): map every request sentence to promises and concrete scenarios (GIVEN data, WHEN input, THEN result) BEFORE reading code; then look for CONFLICTS between each sentence and what the system already does (read code only for that); a conflict is a question for the owner, or, when absent, your recorded decision. Then write the tests from the public interface, in the user's words, and record the mapping, the edge matrix, the laws, the messages, your predictions and the RED run in the same record. Each test named in `mapping` or `covers_scenarios` must exist in a test file of the clone at this point; the tool refuses a name no test file defines.
- Tests: only through the public port (CLI, HTTP, public API), no process ids, no production symbols. For each test note the nearest plausible WRONG behaviour it would still accept and tighten the test when that behaviour violates a promise. Every design law becomes a generated property: its generator is a test decorated with @given, so the tool checks it is a property test. A generator written as a node id (tests/test_x.py::test_y) is read as its name. No test depends on the wall clock to decide its outcome or on the order of a set; when the product stamps whole seconds, assert relations between stamps, never exact times. Unit tests up to 10 ms, integration tests seconds; a slow test is a defect, fix it now.
- A concurrency, crash or kill test must PROVE ITS PREMISE from the outside: observe that the overlap or interruption really happened through what the public port and the files the product writes show, and assert the exact before-or-after state. A test that would pass whether or not the premise occurred proves nothing. A test hook inside production code is a design decision: record it and its reason, never a default.
- Seen RED first: the tool runs the `red` command itself on the pre-feature tree, with this change's test files copied over it, and records the test ids that fail there. A RED with no failing test id, such as an import error or an argparse usage error, is refused. So is a RED in which a test named in `mapping` or `covers_scenarios` passes on the pre-feature tree: such a test is vacuous, whatever its assertion. Make every promise's test fail as an assertion on the behaviour the promise names.
- Predictions: before craft, state in `predictions` what each probe case you will explore should show on the pre-feature tree (`ID => base held` or `ID => base differs`). The probe compares it with what it observes; a surprise is shown in the brief, and it is information, not a failure. Explore scenarios that change the diff: a case that exposes a behaviour you had not thought of ends as a test or code line of the change, and you name it with `artifact=` on the case.
- Design: search for the existing owner of every rule first (`owners`), reuse it, and create new code only with a reason. One business rule, one owner. Record the paradigm, the boundaries, the domain types and the invariants with their witness; the invariant witness is checked after craft.
- Prefactoring (decided in design, mandatory): record `prefactoring` as yes or no with `prefactoring_reason`. Yes means the existing code the change touches must be prepared first: make each behaviour-preserving refactor a SEPARATE commit BEFORE any feature commit, with no test edited, and the same check green on the commit before it and on the commit itself (the tool runs both). Then record craft's `refactor_commits` (those commits, oldest first) with `refactor_check` (the check). No means no refactor commit at all. The tool refuses a yes without commits and a no with commits.
- Model: record `formal_model` before craft: `modeled` or `not_needed`, with the reason the tool checks against the added lines after craft. A modeled decision also needs `mutant_file`, a mutant of the model with one deliberate defect, and `mutant_command`, the checker run on it; the checker must reject the mutant (non-zero exit), and that rejection is what the record shows. `quint run` is a bounded random simulation: the record says so as a warning, and a pass there is no violation within the bound, not a proof. `quint verify` needs an Apalache backend; when apalache-mc is not on PATH, say so in `not_verified` instead of implying an exhaustive check.
- Code: implement through the public port to GREEN. Decisions live in rich owners (value object, entity, aggregate), not in services or scripts. Paradigm and file layout are those named in the authority text; when it names none, mirror the sibling code; no duplication; one source of truth.
- Behaviour you were not asked to change stays: do not break or widen existing public behaviour (reserved names, existing statuses, existing ordering, existing commands) without a recorded owner decision. The request's own sentences outrank any compatibility heuristic; when keeping an old name valid would make the requested command unavailable in some case, that is a conflict for the owner.
- A command you add beside an existing sibling does each cross-cutting thing the way the sibling does (when it reads the clock relative to taking the lock, how it saves, how it reports errors, what it stamps); read the sibling first and treat every difference as a recorded decision.
- Every option a command shows in `--help` must work, and every option that works is shown. A new reserved word or verb never makes a name that was valid before invalid.
- Commit the change in your clone (tests, code, documents) before `probe` and `selfreview`: the probe's preserve cases, its artifacts and the contract checks read the committed change from the base to HEAD. Leave no uncommitted change at the end.
- Commits: each commit has one purpose; tests, code and documents go in separate commits. The subject is `type(scope): subject`, at most 100 characters (types: feat, fix, refactor, test, docs, chore). Commit only the files the request needs: no scratch file (the mutant file the model step requires is part of the change and is committed with the model), no change to a file the request does not name (a .gitignore line, for example). Run the green check after each commit and keep its output.
- Documents hold only current operational text: no narration of run output, no count or timing you could derive, no copy of a model or a test run. Cite a test, a model or a receipt by its path. Name the type of each document (tutorial, how-to, reference or explanation).
- Every changed hunk traces to a request sentence or to a recorded decision. A defect you find outside the request, or an extra option, is reported to the owner in the handoff, not added to the diff.
- Probe cases: kind=new is a case whose result differs on the pre-feature tree (baseline-contains=TEXT names what the old tree prints instead); kind=preserve is behaviour that must stay as it was (protects=PATH names the test or file that guards it). A case whose result is the same on both trees is kind=preserve, never kind=new.
- Scorecard: the verdict the record gives compares your findings with the pre-feature tree. Read the verdict, not an exit code alone: a non-zero exit that adds no finding is the pre-existing state, and a clean verdict needs no argument.
- Stale steps: a record of a step that replays (`replay` in the schema) records its stale dependents again in the same call, in schema order, and prints each `replayed` line; nothing else is needed. If a record still reports stale steps, a replayed step was refused: the refusal names the step and the correction; record that step again with the correction and the rest replays. Do not rebuild the records by hand. `nwstep replay --workspace WS --root ROOT` runs the same replay on its own.
- Review of yourself: `selfreview` asks 17 dimensions; fix debt now and record `fixed:` with evidence, or justify `n/a:`. An independent reviewer and a blind examiner will check you afterwards; you may record an independent review item in `independent_review` when one was made by someone other than you.
- Documents: after the last record, generate them with `brief --out {{root}}/docs/<short-feature-name>` and commit them with the change. The summary, decisions and trace are derived from the journal; the handoff summary and the not-verified list are the only sentences you write. The reviewer reads only the repository.
- Every guarantee a document states names the test function that establishes it; the tool checks each guarantee-style sentence of the handoff summary against the committed change, and every sentence that names no test of the change, or a file or symbol the change does not define, is moved to the 'Not backed by the diff' section. Write the smaller claim when the test establishes less. Documents say exactly what a test proves and what it does not (a kill while a lock is held is not a kill during the write; a sample of runs is not a proof).
- Skills come first. Before you record a step, read EVERY skill that `nwstep next` lists for it, each in full, in one read (no head, no limit, no offset). Then write down, in your notes, the actions from those skills that you will apply in this step. A step recorded before its listed skills were read is a step to redo.

## STEP OUTPUTS
- oracle (one record, before any code): `request`, `promises`, `sentence_map`, `scenarios`, `conflicts`, `decisions` (a `S2: decision` line for every CONFLICT sentence whose promise is recorded here, and `DEC1: decision and reason` for other decisions), `mapping` (`P1 => test`), `covers_scenarios` (`SC1 => test`), `predictions` (`SC1 => base held` or `SC1 => base differs`, for the cases you will explore), `interfaces`, `matrix` (every interface with every edge category), `laws` (`LAW1 | law | generator=TEST_ID | observation=TEXT | mutant=TEXT` per design law, the generator decorated with @given, or `LAW0 | none: reason`), `messages` (`MSG1 | input | what | why | how | test=TEST_ID` per edge message, or `MSG0 | none: reason`), and `red` (the test command that must fail on the pre-feature tree). Before craft the tool checks only the format of laws and messages; their test names are checked after craft.
- design: `owners` (`rule => file that owns it`, or `rule => new: reason`), `reuse_decision`, `paradigm`, `boundaries`, `domain_types`, `invariants` (`INV1 | invariant | witness=PATH:SYMBOL | bypass=text or none`, or `INV0 | none: reason`), `prefactoring` (`yes` or `no`) and `prefactoring_reason`.
- model: `formal_model`. `modeled` needs `model_file` (a `.qnt` or `.tla` file the change contains), `check_command` (its exit code is recorded), `invariant_witnesses` (`INV1 | model invariant name | witness=PATH:SYMBOL`), `mutant_file` (a mutant of the model that differs from it) and `mutant_command` (the same checker on the mutant; it must exit non-zero). `not_needed` needs `no_state_reason` of at least 30 characters that names the change's state, protocol, concurrency or crash window keywords.
- craft: `refactor_commits` and `refactor_check` only when design decided `prefactoring` yes. Then `green` (the test command with --durations=10, must pass) and `scorecard` (lint, types and the other mechanical checks, compared with the pre-feature tree). Each attempt is a revision.
- Commit the change, then `probe`: `cases` (`I1.empty | command | exit=0,kind=new,baseline-contains=TEXT` or `I1.empty | command | exit=0,kind=preserve,protects=PATH`, each optionally with `,artifact=NAME` for a function or test the committed change defines) for every matrix cell that is not n/a.
- selfreview: `answers` for D1 to D17 (`D1: pass - evidence`, `D1: fixed:evidence` or `D1: n/a:reason`). For D4, D5, D9, D12 and D15 pass or fixed cites a PATH:LINE of an added line, and n/a is not accepted. Selfreview also verifies, against the committed change, the oracle laws and messages, the design invariants and the model witnesses and reason. A refusal there names the step to correct: record that step again, then record selfreview again. `independent_review` is optional.
- handoff: `summary` (what was delivered, how it was checked, and what the reader must know; each guarantee names its test function) and `not_verified` (everything you did not verify, or `none`). The generated summary also carries the observed side effects and the commit list from git, the explored scenarios with their predictions and surprises, and the sentences the diff does not back.

